&#8220;Dad, the good mood ran out! What do I do?&#8221;
&#8220;Happy New Year, Sensei!&#8221;
&#8220;I gave it some thought and changed my mind.&#8221;
&#8220;People ask me, &#8216;You don&#8217;t drink? Then what do you do?&#8217;&#8221;
&#8220;Dude, write a book.&#8221;
&#8220;I&#8217;ll be more intentional with my time. Thanks!&#8221;
&#8220;Since Thailand I haven&#8217;t been able to stop thinking.&#8221;



A year ago I went all in on my own insights and the will of the universe. It was also a chance to test whether any of it holds up. A year later I&#8217;m still rolling around the world. Preaching Buddhism. Teaching meditation. Helping people who are looking for the light. Making up a new reality and rolling right into it.

We ignore what we don&#8217;t know, so let&#8217;s start with something you can measure. Once I understood what I&#8217;m writing about here and started practicing and meditating, my resting heart rate fell almost at once, from 70 to 51. My watch started warning me it was too low. All from realizing that none of this is happening to me.

Freediving came later, and it taught me more ways to step out from under my character&#8217;s control. Put your face in cold water and your heart slows by itself. That&#8217;s the dive reflex, and every mammal has it. Freediving also raised my heart rate variability: how flexibly the heart shifts its pace from one beat to the next. The higher it is, the faster the body moves from stress to rest. I calmed down. Calm body, calm mind.

The strangest result is luck. I catch myself saying it out loud: what can I tell you, I&#8217;m always lucky. Things start happening the moment I move. Events stack up so fast they overlap, and I&#8217;m jumping from one to the next. Friends around the world keep inviting me over. Even bad news turns out, a few months later, to be the best thing that could have happened. In a happy universe things tend to work out somehow, because there&#8217;s no such thing as failure.

The method works. I lead people down the path I walked myself. I didn&#8217;t expect it to work this well. The veil slides off right before my eyes. Everyone starts remembering who they are. All day we play a game: &#8220;How does this day get even better?&#8221; And the universe keeps delivering. I hate to end the day. Something else might still show up.

Proof of work is just another word for the proof that it works.

The best I can do is pass it on.

There are many lives like it, but this one is yours.
